## When breathing becomes a treatment

Most guests come to Mariánské Lázně for their backs, their joints or their metabolism. Yet the respiratory tract has been part of the town's healing repertoire from the beginning — it forms an officially recognised indication group of its own. **Spa treatment for the respiratory tract** here rests on a combination that is hard to assemble anywhere else: a mineral water suited to inhalation, naturally occurring carbon dioxide, peat, and a climate that counts as a healing resource in its own right.

Autumn may be the best time of year for this particular cure. The pollen season is winding down, the town quietens after the summer, and the air in this forest-ringed valley has the damp freshness that mucous membranes dried out by air conditioning and the start of the heating season are crying out for. If you are weighing up an [autumn stay](/en/autumn), this is its healthiest version.

## A climate with a certificate

Mariánské Lázně sits at roughly 630 metres above sea level, in a basin enclosed by the woods of the Slavkovský les protected landscape area. The submontane climate here is described as tonic: it gently stimulates and hardens the body without exhausting it. For the airways, what matters most is what the valley lacks — dust, smog and the hot, dry summer air of the lowlands.

Since 2023 this has been official. The town was granted the status of a climatic spa, the sixth in the Czech Republic and the second in the Karlovy Vary region. It followed air-quality measurements by the Czech Hydrometeorological Institute, which ran at regular intervals throughout the year from May 2021; according to the town, the recorded values fell well below the limits set by Czech legislation. It is the combination of mineral waters, peat, natural spring gas and certified air that makes [climate therapy](/en/climate-therapy) here rather more than a walk in the woods.

## The Forest Spring, and why it is the one you inhale

Of the town's forty-plus springs, one holds a special place where breathing is concerned: the **Forest Spring** (Lesní pramen). Its water is heavily saturated with carbon dioxide and rich in bicarbonate and sodium — the components balneology has long associated with loosening mucus and helping mucous membranes recover. According to the analysis published by the town's official tourism portal, the water contains 2,960 mg/l of carbon dioxide, 1,352 mg/l of bicarbonate and 549 mg/l of sodium, with total mineralisation of around 2,752 mg/l.

The spring is used for drinking cures, for gargling and — the point here — for inhalation, chiefly in disorders of the upper respiratory tract and the digestive system. Its neoclassical pavilion stands away from the main promenade, by the Třebízský brook in the wooded part of the spa district; the walk out to it is a small terrain cure in itself. One caveat about the pavilion: according to the town portal it is currently temporarily closed, while the original outflow of the spring stays freely accessible. Check the current position before you set off.

Forest Spring water is also piped directly into several spa houses — among them the Nové Lázně and Pacifik hotels of the Ensana group — so inhalations and drinking cures do not end when the pavilion closes. The full profile of the spring, and where to find it, is in the [Forest Spring entry](/en/springs-overview/lesni).

